+//! Leases: what a claim costs when the worker holding it goes away.
+//!
+//! `relay/2.5-atomic-claim` made a claim a *lease* — `lease_expires_at` says when a worker that
+//! stopped answering loses its rows — and this step is the other half of that sentence: who reads
+//! that instant, and what a worker that is stopping on purpose does instead of waiting for it.
+//!
+//! There are three ways a row in `claimed` stops being worked on, and they are not the same:
+//!
+//! | how it ended | what the row holds | what this module does |
+//! | --- | --- | --- |
+//! | the attempt answered | a record, written by the worker | nothing: the row is `sent` or `owed` |
+//! | the process was killed | nobody, until the lease runs out | [`reclaim_expired`] gives it back |
+//! | the process was asked to stop | an attempt cut short by the drain deadline | [`release_held`] gives it back |
+//!
+//! The middle row is why a claim is a lease and not a lock: `kill -9` cannot be handled, so the only
+//! thing that can recover the row is the instant written on it. The bottom row is why a lease is not
+//! the *only* thing that can recover a row: a worker that is stopping knows it is stopping, and it
+//! knows which rows it holds — so a rollout costs seconds rather than a lease, and the difference is
+//! the difference between a deploy that looks like an outage and one nobody notices.
+//!
+//! Three shapes here are decisions rather than taste:
+//!
+//! * **stopping is a sequence, and the sequence is the point.** Stop *claiming* first (a worker that
+//!   claims while it is draining is a worker that takes on work it will not finish), then give the
+//!   attempts in flight a deadline to answer ([`crate::concurrency::Gate::quiesce`]), then hand back
+//!   whatever is left. Each step is observable, and each one can be got wrong on its own;
+//! * **the drain deadline is a budget, not a hope.** The subscriber on the other side may answer in
+//!   a second or in a minute, and the orchestrator that sent `SIGTERM` has a `SIGKILL` for the
+//!   process that will not stop — the deadline has to be shorter than that, and the work it cuts has
+//!   to be *given back* rather than left to expire;
+//! * **a reclaimed row is not a lease that was given back.** They differ in exactly one number:
+//!   [`reclaim_expired`] counts the attempt, because nobody knows whether the subscriber was reached,
+//!   and [`release_held`] does not, because the attempt it interrupts was never allowed to finish
+//!   *and* because the row is going back to the queue deliberately. Counting nothing in the first
+//!   case is how a delivery whose worker keeps dying is retried for ever; counting in the second is
+//!   how a rollout makes a delivery look like it failed.
+//!
+//! **And the boundary that has to be said out loud**: this is `async/graceful-shutdown@apply` —
+//! `mailer/1.14-worker` — carried one level down. There, stopping was "stop accepting, drain what is
+//! in memory, exit inside a deadline", and the thing at risk was a message in a `VecDeque`: dropping
+//! it lost a confirmation email for ever. Here the same sequence is run over work that is *claimed in
+//! a database*, and the cost of dropping a task has changed shape: the delivery is not lost, it
+//! becomes **invisible for the length of the lease** — the row is `claimed` by a process that no
+//! longer exists, and only the instant on it can say when the work comes back. That is why the drain
+//! deadline and the lease are two numbers and not one: the first decides how much work this process
+//! abandons, the second decides how long the queue hides the damage.
+
+use std::future::Future;
+use std::pin::pin;
+use std::time::Duration;
+
+use sqlx::PgPool;
+
+use crate::concurrency::{Report, Worker};
+use crate::error::Error;
+
+/// How long a worker that has nothing to do waits before asking the queue again.
+///
+/// A worker that claims as fast as the database can answer is a worker that spends a core on an empty
+/// queue, and the queue's own answer to "nothing" is already a query. The wait is short enough that a
+/// delivery that arrives during it is attempted promptly, and long enough that an idle process is
+/// idle.
+const IDLE: Duration = Duration::from_millis(200);
+
+/// Give back every row whose lease has run out, so the work it holds is claimable again.
+///
+/// This is the recovery `kill -9` needs and cannot ask for: the process is gone, nothing will write
+/// its rows down, and the only record of it that survives is `lease_expires_at`. The row goes back to
+/// `pending`, due now, with the delivery id, the event and the attempt count it had — so the
+/// subscriber is told about it again, which is a duplicate, which is what
+/// `relay/2.6-at-least-once` says this service pays for a crash in the window between sending and
+/// recording.
+///
+/// The attempt is counted. Nobody observed what happened to it: it may have been sent, it may never
+/// have left. Not counting it would mean a delivery whose worker keeps dying is attempted for ever
+/// (the failure count is the only thing that ends a delivery), and counting it is the reading that
+/// keeps the retry budget honest — `relay/2.10-dlq` is where a delivery that kills its worker
+/// belongs, and it can only get there if this is counted.
+///
+/// The statement is one `update` over the rows a partial index holds
+/// (`migrations/0007_lease_reclaim.sql`), and it writes nothing when there is nothing to reclaim.
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// Returns [`Error::Database`] when the update cannot be made.
